WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.262
A multi-cloud agency runs several stacks:
Azure Update Manager, AWS Systems Manager,

2
00:00:06.262 --> 00:00:11.663
a virtualization lifecycle manager,
firewall syslog, a Linux host agent.

3
00:00:11.663 --> 00:00:16.050
Five partial views can't prove one
vulnerability is fixed everywhere.

4
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.014
The program's answer is
an evidence contract:

5
00:00:19.014 --> 00:00:24.462
one record shape every source emits, with
eight fields fixed before any data flows.

6
00:00:24.462 --> 00:00:28.886
They carry the six things NIST's
AU-3 asks a record to establish,

7
00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:33.050
plus the control it evidences,
a verdict and a disposition.

8
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.825
The join key is authoritative
asset identity plus time.

9
00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:39.113
Every stack has its own identifier.

10
00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:42.524
Even Microsoft's normalization
model lists separate

11
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:46.087
device IDs per product and
calls a hostname a last resort.

12
00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:50.512
So the key comes from the naming plane,
which has to exist before the pipeline.

13
00:00:50.512 --> 00:00:53.062
And time means when
the state was observed,

14
00:00:53.062 --> 00:00:56.413
because arrival time joins
the wrong records silently.

15
00:00:56.413 --> 00:01:01.138
One caution: the system-wide,
time-correlated trail,

16
00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:04.176
AU-12(1), is a High-baseline control.

17
00:01:04.176 --> 00:01:07.180
At Moderate, the program
selects it by design.
